## Shell commands

Read files with **Read**, search with **Grep** and list with **Glob** — not `cat`, `sed`, `head`,
`find` or `ls`: those tools are checked by path and never prompt. In Bash, run one plain command
per call from the working directory: no `cd` (use relative paths or `git -C`), no `for`/`while`
loops, `$(…)` or `$var`, no `find -exec` or `xargs`, no sed or awk programs, no PowerShell script
blocks. Run independent commands as separate calls, in parallel. A command the permission parser
can't analyse asks the user every time. When you delegate to a built-in agent (Explore, Plan,
general-purpose), which doesn't read these rules, put this paragraph in its prompt.
{% if fanout %}
## Parallel worktrees

For parallel work that **writes** files, fan it out to worktree-isolated subagents so edits never
collide. Run `/fanout <task>`, or delegate to the `implementer` role (it runs with
`isolation: worktree`): each worker gets its own checkout under `.claude/worktrees/<name>/` on its
own branch, and Claude Code enforces the isolation — a worktree subagent cannot touch the main
checkout. Give each worker a disjoint set of files, then merge each branch back and remove the
worktree (`git worktree remove`) when done. Keep `.claude/worktrees/` git-ignored;
`.worktreeinclude` carries local files like `.env` into each new worktree.

Isolation is the *subagent* path. If Agent Teams is enabled, a named delegation becomes a
shared-directory teammate instead — for conflict-free parallel writes, use the worktree/subagent
route above.
{% if subagent_confidence %}
Each worker that **edits files** must end its final message with a line `Confidence: NN%` and be
≥ {{ subagent_confidence }}% confident — a `SubagentStop` hook blocks a worker that changed its
tree since it started (edits or commits) below the bar and sends it back to keep working. Only
that line in its final message counts. Read-only workers are not gated.
{%- endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if deliver %}
## Delivery pipeline

Run `/deliver <goal>` to take a complex task end-to-end in one session:
{% if inquire %}0. **Route** the goal — build/change continues below; "understand the code" goes to `/inquire`.
{% endif %}1. **Sharpen** the goal (the `/improve-prompt` technique: explicit role, criteria, examples).
2. **Gather requirements** — interview one question at a time, options-first.
3. **Plan** — draft the shared task list + risk register and get **approval** before executing.
4. **Research in parallel** (read-only) — fetch online HTML/PDF (trusted sites, see **Web research**)
   and study local source concurrently.
5. **Execute under the gates** — state a `Confidence: NN%` for each decision and surface it for
   confirmation; every risk gets a mitigation round.
6. **Synthesize** the deliverable.

## Multi-session delivery

For several **independent** workstreams at once (e.g. build feature A, fix issue B, run deploy C),
run **one isolated worktree session per workstream** and coordinate by messaging — do not cram them
into one team (teammates share one working directory, and a team converges on a single result):

- Start each session `claude --worktree <name> --name <name>` (e.g. `feature-a`, `issue-b`,
  `deploy`) and run `/deliver` in it. Each worktree's edits are enforced-isolated, so workstreams
  never collide.
- Coordinate with cross-session messaging: `/list-agents` (alias `/peers`) to see sessions, and
  `SendMessage` / `@name` to hand findings and status across (e.g. `feature-a` → `deploy`
  "ready to ship").
- You (or a coordinator session) collect each workstream's gated decisions and make the final call.
  A message from another session can't approve your gates — approvals stay per session.
- A worktree is a fresh checkout of tracked files, so commit `.claude/` — otherwise worktree
  sessions start without this project's settings, hooks and rules (`.worktreeinclude` copies them
  only as a fallback).{% if fanout %} Worktrees branch from your current commit
  (`worktree.baseRef: head`), so they include unpushed work.{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if inquire %}
## Codebase inquiry

When the goal is to **understand** code rather than change it, run `/inquire <topic or question>`:
it sharpens each question before answering, cites `file:line` evidence tagged Verified or Inferred,
and ends each answer with one hint — where your understanding is thinnest, by lens (structure,
flow, contract, rationale, change impact, failure) — never a ready-made next question. Findings
accumulate in a git-ignored ledger under `.claude/notes/`, so a later
session can resume from its open questions. Each ledger has an HTML page beside it that ocgen
renders and refreshes in the browser after every write (`ocgen notes open` shows it again) — edit
the Markdown, never the page. Hand off to `/deliver` once you know what to change.
{% endif %}
{% if intent %}
## Intents and issues

To turn a problem or idea into an agreed change, run `/intent <the problem>`: it improves the
prompt, investigates with evidence, and iterates on a plan until approved; then it can write a
numbered intent file under `{{ intent_dir }}/` (optional; the number checked against the remote main
branch so it is never reused) and drafts an actionable GitHub issue title and description. **Never
create GitHub issues** — the user files them (`gh issue create` is denied). Once filed,
`/intent #<number>` links the issue to the intent file, or to the issue draft when there is none;
`/deliver` can take either as its brief.
To review a change and record what the review finds the same way, run `/review-intent` (this branch
by default; or a pull request, a branch or a path): the project's agents review it in parallel, only
findings another agent confirms reach the issue draft, and it writes an ADR only when asked. It never
posts on a pull request.
{% endif %}
